5. Bean Lifecycle

52. What is bean lifecycle?
-->Answer:
      
      The Bean Lifecycle is the sequence of steps a Spring Bean goes through from creation to destruction.
      
      In simple words:
      
      Spring creates the bean → injects dependencies → initializes it → uses it → destroys it.
      
      Basic flow:
      
      Bean Instantiation
             ↓
      Dependency Injection
             ↓
      BeanPostProcessor (Before Initialization)
             ↓
      Initialization
             ↓
      BeanPostProcessor (After Initialization)
             ↓
      Bean is Ready
             ↓
      Destroy








53. What are the phases of bean lifecycle?
-->The major phases are:

      Instantiation – Spring creates the bean object.
      Dependency Injection – Spring injects required dependencies.
      Pre-initialization – BeanPostProcessor works before initialization.
      Initialization – initialization methods are executed.
      Post-initialization – BeanPostProcessor works after initialization.
      Bean Ready – bean is available for use.
      Destruction – Spring destroys the bean when the container shuts down.
      Instantiation
           ↓
      Dependency Injection
           ↓
      Pre-Initialization
           ↓
      Initialization
           ↓
      Post-Initialization
           ↓
      Ready to Use
           ↓
      Destruction







54. What happens during bean instantiation?
-->Instantiation means creating the bean object.

      Spring creates the object using its constructor.
      
      For example:
      
      @Component
      public class EmployeeService {
      
          public EmployeeService() {
              System.out.println("Constructor called");
          }
      }
      
      When Spring creates this bean:
      
      Spring Container
            ↓
      Calls EmployeeService constructor
            ↓
      EmployeeService object created
      
      So:
      
      During instantiation, Spring creates the bean object in memory.










55. What is dependency injection phase?
-->After creating the bean, Spring provides the bean's required dependencies.
      
      Example:
      
      @Component
      public class EmployeeService {
      
          private EmployeeRepository repository;
      
          @Autowired
          public EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository repository) {
              this.repository = repository;
          }
      }
      
      Here:
      
      Spring creates EmployeeRepository
                 ↓
      Spring creates EmployeeService
                 ↓
      Spring injects EmployeeRepository
                 ↓
      EmployeeService is ready for initialization
      
      Interview answer:
            Dependency Injection is the phase where Spring provides the required dependencies to the newly created bean.









56. What is initialization phase?
-->The initialization phase is the stage where Spring performs the final setup of a bean after its dependencies have been injected.

You can perform initialization using:
      @PostConstruct
      InitializingBean
      Custom init-method

Example:
      @Component
      public class EmployeeService {
      
          @PostConstruct
          public void init() {
              System.out.println("Bean initialized");
          }
      }
      
      The init() method executes after dependency injection.
      
      Constructor
           ↓
      Dependency Injection
           ↓
      @PostConstruct
           ↓
      Bean Ready










57. What is destroy phase?
-->The destroy phase occurs when the Spring container is shutting down and Spring is removing/destroying beans.

            It allows you to perform cleanup operations such as:
                  Closing database connections
                  Closing files
                  Releasing resources
                  Closing network connections
                  
            Example:
                  @PreDestroy
                  public void cleanup() {
                      System.out.println("Cleaning resources...");
                  }
            
            Interview answer:
                  The destroy phase is the final phase of a bean's lifecycle where Spring performs cleanup before removing the bean.








58. What is initialization method?
-->An initialization method is a method executed after dependency injection and before the bean is ready to use.

Example:
      @PostConstruct
      public void init() {
          System.out.println("Initialization method");
      }

Another approach:   
      public void init() {
          // initialization logic
      }
      
      and configure it as an init-method.

Purpose:
      Create bean
           ↓
      Inject dependencies
           ↓
      Initialize bean
           ↓
      Use bean








59. What is destroy method?
-->A destroy method is a method executed when Spring destroys the bean.

Example:
      @PreDestroy
      public void destroy() {
          System.out.println("Bean destroyed");
      }
      
      It is generally used for cleanup.


Application Running
       ↓
Bean Used
       ↓
Application Shutdown
       ↓
Destroy Method









60. What is @PostConstruct?
-->@PostConstruct is used to execute a method after dependency injection is completed.

Example:
      @Component
      public class PaymentService {
      
          @PostConstruct
          public void init() {
              System.out.println("PaymentService initialized");
          }
      }

The order is approximately:

      Constructor
          ↓
      Dependency Injection
          ↓
      @PostConstruct
          ↓
      Bean Ready

Important interview point:
      @PostConstruct is commonly used for initialization logic that requires the bean's dependencies to already be available.








61. What is @PreDestroy?
-->@PreDestroy is used to execute a method before Spring destroys the bean.

Example:
      @Component
      public class DatabaseService {
      
          @PreDestroy
          public void cleanup() {
              System.out.println("Closing resources");
          }
      }

Flow:
      Bean is being destroyed
              ↓
      @PreDestroy method
              ↓
      Bean removed

Important:
      @PreDestroy is generally used for cleanup operations.










62. Constructor vs init method?
-->This is a very important interview question.
| Constructor                                                | Init Method                         |
| ---------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Used to create the object                                  | Used to initialize the object       |
| Executes during instantiation                              | Executes after dependency injection |
| Creates the bean instance                                  | Performs initialization logic       |
| Dependencies may be supplied through constructor injection | Dependencies are already available  |
| Executes before `@PostConstruct`                           | Executes during initialization      |

Example:
      @Component
      public class EmployeeService {
      
          public EmployeeService() {
              System.out.println("Constructor");
          }
      
          @PostConstruct
          public void init() {
              System.out.println("Init method");
          }
      }

Output:
      Constructor
      Init method

Simple interview answer:
      The constructor is mainly used for object creation, whereas the initialization method is used to perform initialization after
      dependency injection.










63. What is BeanPostProcessor?
-->BeanPostProcessor is a Spring interface that allows us to perform custom processing on beans before and after initialization.

It provides two important methods:

postProcessBeforeInitialization()

and

postProcessAfterInitialization()

Example:

@Component
public class MyBeanPostProcessor
        implements BeanPostProcessor {

    @Override
    public Object postProcessBeforeInitialization(
            Object bean, String beanName) {

        System.out.println("Before initialization: " + beanName);
        return bean;
    }

    @Override
    public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(
            Object bean, String beanName) {

        System.out.println("After initialization: " + beanName);
        return bean;
    }
}










64. How BeanPostProcessor works?
-->This is where you should remember the exact sequence.

Suppose we have:

@Component
public class EmployeeService {

    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        System.out.println("PostConstruct");
    }
}

Spring roughly follows:

1. Constructor
       ↓
2. Dependency Injection
       ↓
3. BeanPostProcessor
   Before Initialization
       ↓
4. @PostConstruct
       ↓
5. Initialization method
       ↓
6. BeanPostProcessor
   After Initialization
       ↓
7. Bean Ready
Easy way to remember
Constructor
    ↓
DI
    ↓
BEFORE
    ↓
INIT
    ↓
AFTER
    ↓
READY
Example
@Component
public class MyBeanPostProcessor
        implements BeanPostProcessor {

    @Override
    public Object postProcessBeforeInitialization(
            Object bean, String beanName) {

        System.out.println("Before: " + beanName);
        return bean;
    }

    @Override
    public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(
            Object bean, String beanName) {

        System.out.println("After: " + beanName);
        return bean;
    }
}

If the bean has:

@PostConstruct
public void init() {
    System.out.println("Init");
}

The conceptual order is:

Constructor
      ↓
Dependency Injection
      ↓
Before Initialization
      ↓
@PostConstruct
      ↓
After Initialization
      ↓
Bean Ready
⭐ Interview definition

BeanPostProcessor is a Spring extension mechanism used to modify or process beans before and after their initialization.

Why is it important?

Spring itself uses post-processors for many features. They are involved in processing beans for things such as annotations, proxies, and other container functionality.

🔥 Most Important Lifecycle Sequence for Interview

You should memorize this:

          Bean Lifecycle
               │
               ↓
        Bean Instantiation
               │
               ↓
       Dependency Injection
               │
               ↓
 BeanPostProcessor - BEFORE
               │
               ↓
         @PostConstruct
               │
               ↓
      Initialization Method
               │
               ↓
 BeanPostProcessor - AFTER
               │
               ↓
          Bean Ready
               │
               ↓
       Application Runs
               │
               ↓
          @PreDestroy
               │
               ↓
       Destroy Method
