⭐ One-line answers to remember

52. Bean Lifecycle:

The sequence of stages a Spring bean goes through from creation to destruction.

53. Phases:

Instantiation, dependency injection, initialization, post-processing, usage, and destruction.

54. Instantiation:

Spring creates the bean object, usually by calling its constructor.

55. Dependency Injection:

Spring injects the required dependencies into the bean.

56. Initialization:

Spring performs initialization after dependencies are injected.

57. Destroy:

Spring performs cleanup before destroying the bean.

58. Initialization Method:

A method executed after dependency injection to initialize the bean.

59. Destroy Method:

A method executed before the bean is destroyed for cleanup.

60. @PostConstruct:

Executes a method after dependency injection and before the bean is ready.

61. @PreDestroy:

Executes a method before Spring destroys the bean.

62. Constructor vs Init:

Constructor creates the object; init method performs initialization after dependency injection.

63. BeanPostProcessor:

A Spring interface used to process beans before and after initialization.

64. How it works:

It processes a bean before initialization and again after initialization, allowing Spring or developers to customize bean processing.









